<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-app-trig-standard-identities" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>The Basics of Verifying Trigonometric Identities</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-app-trig-identity">
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <term>identity</term> is an equation that is true for any value of any variable that appears in the equation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-app-trig-fundamental-identities">
        <title>The Fundamental Trigonometric Identities</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Reciprocal Identities
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\csc(x)}\amp \cos(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\sec(x)}\amp \tan(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\cot(x)}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\csc(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\sin(x)}\amp \sec(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\cos(x)}\amp \cot(x)\amp=\dfrac{1}{\tan(x)}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Quotient Identities
                <md>
                    <mrow>\tan(x)\amp=\dfrac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}\amp \cot(x)\amp=\dfrac{\cos(x)}{\sin(x)}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Pythagorean Identities
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos^2(x)+\sin^2(x)\amp=1</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>1+\tan^2(x)\amp=\sec^2(x)</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\cot^2(x)+1\amp=\csc^2(x)</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Parity (Even-Odd) Identities
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(-x)\amp=-\sin(x)\amp \cos(-x)\amp=\cos(x)\amp \tan(-x)\amp=-\tan(x)</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\csc(-x)\amp=-\csc(x)\amp \sec(-x)\amp=\sec(x)\amp \cot(-x)\amp=-\cot(x)</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <definition xml:id="def-app-trig-verify-identity">
        <statement>
            <p>
                To <term>verify an identity</term>, we simplify one side of the equation until it becomes identical to the other side of the equation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <note xml:id="note-app-trig-important-verify">
        <title>Big, Very Important Note</title>
        <p>
            Because it is not known that the equation is true for all values of <m>x</m>, you cannot move terms or factors to the other side of the equation.
            Recall this fact from algebra:
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{If }A=B,\text{ then for any }C,\ A+C=B+C.</mrow>
            </md>
            But this rests on the fact that you already know <m>A=B</m>. If you have not verified an identity, you do not know if <m>A=B</m>. Therefore, when proving an identity, you
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        cannot add or subtract the same expression to both sides of an equation,
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        cannot multiply or divide both sides of an equation by a non-zero number, and
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        cannot apply a function to both sides of an equation.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </note>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-0" workspace="2in">
        <title>Use an Existing Identity</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\tan^2(x)=\dfrac{\sin^2(x)}{\cos^2(x)}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-1" workspace="2in">
        <title>Use an Existing Identity</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\csc(x)\tan(x)=\sec(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-2" workspace="5in">
        <title>Change Everything into Sines and Cosines</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\cos(x)\cot(x)+\sin(x)=\csc(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-3" workspace="3in">
        <title>Use Algebra Techniques</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\sin(x)-\sin(x)\cos^2(x)=\sin^3(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-4" workspace="2in">
        <title>Use More Than One Technique</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\dfrac{1+\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)}=\csc(\theta)+\cot(\theta)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-5" workspace="7in">
        <title>Combine Fractions</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\dfrac{\sin(x)}{1+\cos(x)}+\dfrac{1+\cos(x)}{\sin(x)}=2\csc(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-6" workspace="4in">
        <title>Multiply by "one"</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\dfrac{\cos(x)}{1+\sin(x)}=\dfrac{1-\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-7" workspace="7in">
        <title>Turn into Sines and Cosines</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\dfrac{\sec(x)+\csc(-x)}{\sec(x)\csc(x)}=\sin(x)-\cos(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exa-app-trig-verify-identity-8" workspace="7in">
        <title>Work Both Sides Separately</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the identity <m>\dfrac{1}{1+\sin(\theta)}+\dfrac{1}{1-\sin(\theta)}=2+2\tan^2(\theta)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <note xml:id="note-app-trig-basic-strategies">
        <title>Basic Strategies</title>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Use identities:</alert> this is the most fundamental tool for proving identities. Think of identities as a list of substitutions you are allowed to make. The identities can either be algebraic or trigonometric.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>If you start on one side and get stuck, try working from the other side instead.</alert> Usually, you should start with the more complicated side and try to simplify it. But, sometimes, both sides are equally as complicated. So, if the side you choose to start with is not panning out, try starting with the other side instead.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Work on both sides separately:</alert> If you start on both sides and get stuck, see if each side can be simplified to a third expression. This often reveals why you got stuck (usually a weird substitution or identity you normally would not think to use).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If all else fails, <alert>convert everything to sine and cosine.</alert>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Do not add, subtract, multiply, or divide both sides of the equation by an expression or number.</alert> These rules only work with equations that are assumed to be true, and when proving an identity, you do not know if the equation is true or not.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Practice as many identities as you can.</alert>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </note>
</worksheet>
